  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-12a-2015-p4.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Let \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) be the numbers with \\(x \\gt y \\gt 0.\\) Then \\(x+y = 5(x-y).\\)\n\nExpanding gives \\(x+y = 5x-5y,\\) so \\(6y = 4x\\) and \\[\\dfrac{x}{y} = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\\]\n\nThus, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "Let the numbers be \\(x \\gt y\\) and write \\(x+y=5(x-y)\\)",
        "Rearrange to relate \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), then form \\(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\)"
      ],
